(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_biomoleculeschemistry=[{id:"neet-2026r-q066",pn:66,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Structure of proteins",q:"The correct statement about peptides and proteins is",o:["Only the proteins having a quaternary structure are biologically active","In \u03B2-pleated sheet structures, peptide chains are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds","In \u03B1-helices, the polypeptide chain is twisted into a left-handed screw (helix) through intramolecular hydrogen bonds","Tertiary structure of proteins has two or more polypeptide subunits"],c:1,s:`In \u03B2-pleated sheet structure, peptide chains are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds.
This is the only correct statement.
Statements (1), (3) and (4) are incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2026-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Nucleic acids: DNA and RNA",q:"The correct statement with regard to the secondary structure of DNA/RNA is",o:["DNA possesses a single strand helix structure and contains uracil as one of the four bases","RNA possesses a single strand helix structure and contains thymine as one of the four bases","DNA possesses a double strand helix structure and contains thymine as one of the four bases","RNA possesses a double strand helix structure and contains uracil as one of the four bases"],c:2,s:`RNA is usually single stranded and contains uracil, not thymine.
The secondary structure of DNA is a double-strand helix of two polynucleotide chains.
Its four bases are adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T).`},{id:"neet-2026r-q081",pn:81,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Vitamins and their sources",q:`Match the vitamins in List I with their sources in List II
A. vitamin A \u2014 I. meat
B. vitamin B\u2081\u2082 \u2014 II. sunflower oil
C. vitamin E \u2014 III. green leafy vegetables
D. vitamin K \u2014 IV. carrots
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],c:0,s:`Vitamin A: carrot.
Vitamin B\u2081\u2082: meat.
Vitamin E: sunflower oil.
Vitamin K: green leafy vegetables.
So A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2025-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Vitamins and deficiency diseases",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I (Name of Vitamin) \u2192 List-II (Deficiency disease)
A. Vitamin B\u2081\u2082 \u2014 I. Cheilosis
B. Vitamin D \u2014 II. Convulsions
C. Vitamin B\u2082 \u2014 III. Rickets
D. Vitamin B\u2086 \u2014 IV. Pernicious anaemia
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],c:1,s:`Vitamin B\u2081\u2082: Pernicious anaemia
Vitamin D: Rickets
Vitamin B\u2082: Cheilosis
Vitamin B\u2086: Convulsions`,sfig:{w:329,h:131,alt:"Table: Vitamin B\u2081\u2082 - Pernicious anaemia; Vitamin D - Rickets; Vitamin B\u2082 - Cheilosis; Vitamin B\u2086 - Convulsions.",d:[["r",.5,.5,327,130,""],["l",28.5,.5,28.5,130.5,""],["l",168.5,.5,168.5,130.5,""],["l",.5,34.5,327.5,34.5,""],["l",.5,58.5,327.5,58.5,""],["l",.5,82.5,327.5,82.5,""],["l",.5,106.5,327.5,106.5,""],["t",14.5,21,"","m"],["t",98.5,14.5,"List-I","m"],["t",98.5,27.5,"(Name of Vitamin)","m"],["t",248.5,14.5,"List-II","m"],["t",248.5,27.5,"(Deficiency disease)","m"],["t",6.5,50,"A.",""],["t",34.5,50,"Vitamin B\u2081\u2082",""],["t",174.5,50,"Pernicious anaemia",""],["t",6.5,74,"B.",""],["t",34.5,74,"Vitamin D",""],["t",174.5,74,"Rickets",""],["t",6.5,98,"C.",""],["t",34.5,98,"Vitamin B\u2082",""],["t",174.5,98,"Cheilosis",""],["t",6.5,122,"D.",""],["t",34.5,122,"Vitamin B\u2086",""],["t",174.5,122,"Convulsions",""]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Carbohydrates: fructose",q:`Sugar 'X'
A. is found in honey
B. is a keto sugar
C. exists in \u03B1 and \u03B2 \u2013 anomeric forms.
D. Is laevorotatory.
'X' is :`,o:["D-Glucose","D-Fructose","Maltose","Sucrose"],c:1,s:`D-Fructose is found in honey and is a keto sugar.
It exists in \u03B1 and \u03B2 anomeric forms (\u03B1-D-(\u2013)-fructofuranose and \u03B2-D-(\u2013)-fructofuranose).
It is laevorotatory, so it is written D-(\u2013)-fructose.`,sfig:{w:420,h:200,alt:"Open-chain D-(\u2013)-fructose (CH\u2082OH, C=O, HO\u2013C\u2013H, H\u2013C\u2013OH, H\u2013C\u2013OH, CH\u2082OH) and the \u03B1- and \u03B2-D-(\u2013)-fructofuranose rings.",d:[["t",52,14,"CH\u2082OH","m"],["t",52,44,"C = O","m"],["l",52,18,52,30,""],["t",52,74,"C","m"],["t",46,74,"HO \u2014","e"],["t",58,74,"\u2014 H",""],["l",52,48,52,60,""],["t",52,104,"C","m"],["t",46,104,"H \u2014","e"],["t",58,104,"\u2014 OH",""],["l",52,78,52,90,""],["t",52,134,"C","m"],["t",46,134,"H \u2014","e"],["t",58,134,"\u2014 OH",""],["l",52,108,52,120,""],["t",52,164,"CH\u2082OH","m"],["l",52,138,52,150,""],["t",52,194,"D\u2013(\u2013) \u2014 Fructose","m"],["t",186,44,"O","m"],["l",180,43,150,70,""],["l",192,43,222,70,""],["l",150,70,162,112,""],["l",222,70,210,112,""],["l",162,112,210,112,""],["l",150,70,150,60,""],["t",156,57,"HOH\u2082C","e"],["l",150,70,150,84,""],["t",149,95,"H","m"],["l",162,112,162,96,""],["t",164,94,"H","m"],["l",162,112,162,124,""],["t",162,136,"OH","m"],["l",210,112,210,96,""],["t",213,94,"OH","e"],["l",210,112,210,124,""],["t",210,136,"H","m"],["l",222,70,222,60,""],["t",218,57,"CH\u2082OH",""],["l",222,70,222,84,""],["t",223,96,"OH",""],["t",186,158,"\u03B1\u2013D\u2013(\u2013)\u2013Fructofuranose","ms"],["t",338,44,"O","m"],["l",332,43,302,70,""],["l",344,43,374,70,""],["l",302,70,314,112,""],["l",374,70,362,112,""],["l",314,112,362,112,""],["l",302,70,302,60,""],["t",308,57,"HOH\u2082C","e"],["l",302,70,302,84,""],["t",301,95,"H","m"],["l",314,112,314,96,""],["t",316,94,"H","m"],["l",314,112,314,124,""],["t",314,136,"OH","m"],["l",362,112,362,96,""],["t",365,94,"OH","e"],["l",362,112,362,124,""],["t",362,136,"H","m"],["l",374,70,374,60,""],["t",370,57,"OH",""],["l",374,70,374,84,""],["t",375,96,"CH\u2082OH",""],["t",338,158,"\u03B2\u2013D\u2013(\u2013)\u2013Fructofuranose","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Reactions of glucose",q:`The reagents with which glucose does not react to give the corresponding tests/products are
A. Tollen\u2019s reagent
B. Schiff\u2019s reagent
C. HCN
D. NH\u2082OH
E. NaHSO\u2083
Choose the correct options from the given below :`,o:["B and E","E and D","B and C","A and D"],c:0,s:`Glucose has an aldehyde group, but it does not give Schiff's test.
It also does not form the hydrogen sulphite addition product with NaHSO\u2083.
So the reagents that do not react are B and E.`},{id:"neet-2023-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Nucleosides and nucleotides",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I : A unit formed by the attachment of a base to 1\u2032 position of sugar is known as nucleoside.
Statement-II : When nucleoside is linked to phosphorous acid at 5\u2032-position of sugar moiety, we get nucleotide.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false"],c:3,s:`A base joined to the 1\u2032 position of the sugar makes a nucleoside, so Statement-I is true.
A nucleotide forms when a nucleoside is linked to phosphoric acid at the 5\u2032 position of the sugar.
The question says phosphorous acid, which is not the right acid, so Statement-II is false.
Note: The printed solution text says phosphoric acid and treats both statements as true, yet prints key 4; the question says phosphorous acid, which supports key 4. Printed key kept.`,sfig:{w:680,h:276.3,alt:"Structure of (a) a nucleoside, a five-carbon sugar ring numbered 1\u2032 to 5\u2032 with the base on C1\u2032, and (b) a nucleotide with a phosphate group on the 5\u2032 carbon.",d:[["t",178.5,65.2,"O","m"],["l",110.5,100.3,169.8,66.2,""],["l",246.5,100.3,187.2,66.2,""],["l",246.5,100.3,219.3,163.2,"B"],["l",219.3,163.2,137.7,163.2,"B"],["l",137.7,163.2,110.5,100.3,"B"],["t",110.5,65.2,"H\u2082C","m"],["l",110.5,73.2,110.5,100.3,""],["t",113.9,38,"5\u2032","m"],["t",130.9,107.7,"4\u2032","m"],["t",224.4,106,"1\u2032","m"],["t",159.8,158.7,"3\u2032","m"],["t",197.2,158.7,"2\u2032","m"],["t",98.6,143.4,"H","m"],["l",110.5,100.3,100.3,129.2,""],["t",139.4,128.1,"H","m"],["l",137.7,163.2,137.7,136,""],["t",151.3,209.7,"OH","m"],["l",137.7,163.2,151.3,193.8,""],["t",226.1,209.7,"OH","m"],["l",219.3,163.2,226.1,193.8,""],["t",215.9,126.4,"H","m"],["l",219.3,163.2,215.9,134.3,""],["t",251.6,143.4,"H","m"],["l",246.5,100.3,246.5,127.5,""],["t",268.6,65.2,"Base","m"],["l",246.5,100.3,246.5,74.8,""],["t",34,65.2,"HO","m"],["l",51,61.2,98.6,61.2,""],["t",549.9,65.2,"O","m"],["l",482,100.3,541.3,66.2,""],["l",617.9,100.3,558.6,66.2,""],["l",617.9,100.3,590.8,163.2,"B"],["l",590.8,163.2,509.2,163.2,"B"],["l",509.2,163.2,482,100.3,"B"],["t",482,65.2,"H\u2082C","m"],["l",482,73.2,482,100.3,""],["t",485.4,38,"5\u2032","m"],["t",502.4,107.7,"4\u2032","m"],["t",595.9,106,"1\u2032","m"],["t",531.3,158.7,"3\u2032","m"],["t",568.7,158.7,"2\u2032","m"],["t",470.1,143.4,"H","m"],["l",482,100.3,471.8,129.2,""],["t",510.9,128.1,"H","m"],["l",509.2,163.2,509.2,136,""],["t",522.8,209.7,"OH","m"],["l",509.2,163.2,522.8,193.8,""],["t",597.6,209.7,"OH","m"],["l",590.8,163.2,597.6,193.8,""],["t",587.4,126.4,"H","m"],["l",590.8,163.2,587.4,134.3,""],["t",623.1,143.4,"H","m"],["l",617.9,100.3,617.9,127.5,""],["t",640.1,65.2,"Base","m"],["l",617.9,100.3,617.9,74.8,""],["t",385.1,65.2,"O\u207B","m"],["t",415.7,65.2,"P","m"],["t",448,65.2,"O","m"],["l",393.6,61.2,407.2,61.2,""],["l",424.2,61.2,437.8,61.2,""],["l",458.2,61.2,470.1,61.2,""],["l",415.7,52.7,415.7,22.1,""],["l",410.6,52.7,410.6,22.1,""],["t",415.7,15.9,"O","m"],["l",415.7,71.4,415.7,100.3,""],["t",415.7,116.2,"O\u207B","m"],["t",197.2,259,"(a)","m"],["t",556.8,259,"(b)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Enzymes",q:"The incorrect statement regarding enzymes is",o:["Like chemical catalysts enzymes reduce the activation energy of bio processes.","Enzymes are polysaccharides.","Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and substrate.","Enzymes are biocatalysts."],c:1,s:`Enzymes are made by living cells and act as biocatalysts.
They are protein molecules with a high molecular mass.
They are not polysaccharides, so statement (2) is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q084",pn:84,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Denaturation of proteins",q:"The incorrect statement about denaturation of proteins is :",o:["Uncoiling of the helical structure takes place.","It results due to change of temperature and/ or pH","It results in loss of biological activity of proteins.","A protein is formed from amino acids linked by peptide bonds."],c:3,s:`Statements 1, 2 and 3 describe denaturation correctly.
Statement 4 is about how a protein is built, which has nothing to do with denaturation.
So statement 4 is the one that does not fit.`},{id:"neet-2021-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Vitamins and deficiency diseases",q:"The RBC deficiency is deficiency disease of :",o:["Vitamin B\u2082","Vitamin B\u2081\u2082","Vitamin B\u2086","Vitamin B\u2081"],c:1,s:`B\u2082 (riboflavin): cheilosis and skin burning.
B\u2081\u2082: pernicious anaemia, a shortage of red blood cells.
B\u2086 (pyridoxine): convulsions.
B\u2081 (thiamine): beri-beri.
So the RBC deficiency is due to B\u2081\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2020-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Amino acids",q:"Which of the following is a basic amino acid ?",o:["Alanine","Tyrosine","Lysine","Serine"],c:2,s:`Lysine has two \u2013NH\u2082 groups (one in the side chain) and one \u2013COOH.
More amino groups than carboxyl groups make it a basic amino acid.`,sfig:{w:180,h:150,alt:"Lysine in Fischer form: COOH on top, NH2 left and H right of the central carbon, (CH2)4-NH2 at the bottom, with lone pairs on both nitrogens",d:[["t",80,16,"COOH","m"],["l",65,24,65,96],["l",32,57,103,57],["t",2,62,"NH\u2082"],["c",8,50,1,"F"],["c",13,50,1,"F"],["t",110,62,"H"],["t",56,108,"(CH\u2082)\u2084\u2013NH\u2082"],["c",113,96,1,"F"],["c",118,96,1,"F"],["t",72,138,"Lysine","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2020-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Carbohydrates",q:"Sucrose on hydrolysis gives",o:["\u03B1-D-Glucose + \u03B2-D-Glucose","\u03B1-D-Glucose + \u03B2-D-Fructose","\u03B1-D-Fructose + \u03B2-D-Fructose","\u03B2-D-Glucose + \u03B1-D-Fructose"],c:1,s:`Sucrose is made of \u03B1-D-glucose and \u03B2-D-fructose joined through C1 and C2.
Hydrolysis (with H\u2083O\u207A) splits it back into these two.`},{id:"neet-2019-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Amino acids",q:"The non-essential amino acid among the following is :",o:["alanine","lysine","valine","leucine"],c:0,s:`Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, and lysine, valine and leucine are essential.
Alanine is made in the body, so it is non-essential.`},{id:"neet-2018-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Starch: amylose and amylopectin",q:"The difference between amylose and amylopectin is",o:["Amylopectin have 1 \u2192 4 \u03B1-linkage and 1 \u2192 6 \u03B1-linkage","Amylose have 1 \u2192 4 \u03B1-linkage and 1 \u2192 6 \u03B2-linkage","Amylopectin have 1 \u2192 4 \u03B1-linkage and 1 \u2192 6 \u03B2 linkage","Amylose is made up of glucose and galactose"],c:0,s:`Amylose is a long unbranched chain of \u03B1-D-glucose joined by 1\u21924 \u03B1-glycosidic links.
Amylopectin is branched: main chain has 1\u21924 \u03B1-links and branches start through 1\u21926 \u03B1-links.
Both are made of glucose only, so only option (1) is correct.`,sfig:{w:315,h:195,alt:"Amylopectin: glucose rings joined by alpha-1,4 links in chains, with a branch from carbon 1 through O to CH2 (carbon 6) of a ring in the second chain, an alpha-1,6 link.",d:[["l",62,40,51,59.1,""],["l",51,59.1,29,59.1,""],["l",29,59.1,18,40,""],["l",18,40,29,20.9,""],["l",29,20.9,43,20.9,""],["l",62,40,55,27.9,""],["t",51,24.9,"O","m"],["l",132,40,121,59.1,""],["l",121,59.1,99,59.1,""],["l",99,59.1,88,40,""],["l",88,40,99,20.9,""],["l",99,20.9,113,20.9,""],["l",132,40,125,27.9,""],["t",121,24.9,"O","m"],["l",202,40,191,59.1,""],["l",191,59.1,169,59.1,""],["l",169,59.1,158,40,""],["l",158,40,169,20.9,""],["l",169,20.9,183,20.9,""],["l",202,40,195,27.9,""],["t",191,24.9,"O","m"],["l",62,40,69,40,""],["l",81,40,88,40,""],["t",75,44,"O","m"],["l",132,40,139,40,""],["l",151,40,158,40,""],["t",145,44,"O","m"],["l",95,150,84,169.1,""],["l",84,169.1,62,169.1,""],["l",62,169.1,51,150,""],["l",51,150,62,130.9,""],["l",62,130.9,76,130.9,""],["l",95,150,88,137.9,""],["t",84,134.9,"O","m"],["l",165,150,154,169.1,""],["l",154,169.1,132,169.1,""],["l",132,169.1,121,150,""],["l",121,150,132,130.9,""],["l",132,130.9,146,130.9,""],["l",165,150,158,137.9,""],["t",154,134.9,"O","m"],["l",235,150,224,169.1,""],["l",224,169.1,202,169.1,""],["l",202,169.1,191,150,""],["l",191,150,202,130.9,""],["l",202,130.9,216,130.9,""],["l",235,150,228,137.9,""],["t",224,134.9,"O","m"],["l",95,150,102,150,""],["l",114,150,121,150,""],["t",108,154,"O","m"],["l",165,150,172,150,""],["l",184,150,191,150,""],["t",178,154,"O","m"],["t",105,80,"\u03B1-1,4 linkage","m"],["l",202,40,202,66,""],["t",202,74,"O","m"],["l",202,78,202,95,""],["t",202,108,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",222,108,"6",""],["t",209,58,"1",""],["t",224,80,"\u03B1 linkage",""],["l",202,112,202,131,""],["l",8,150,28,150,""],["t",34,154,"O","m"],["l",40,150,51,150,""],["p","M16 126H10V174H16",""],["l",235,150,256,150,""],["t",262,154,"O","m"],["l",268,150,286,150,""],["p","M280 126H286V174H280",""],["t",292,178,"n","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2018-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Zwitterion of amino acids",q:"Which of the following compounds can form a zwitterion ?",o:["Aniline","Acetanilide","Benzoic acid","Glycine"],c:3,s:`A zwitterion needs both an acidic group (\u2013COOH) and a basic group (\u2013NH\u2082) in one molecule.
Glycine has both: NH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013COOH changes to \u207ANH\u2083\u2013CH\u2082\u2013COO\u207B by internal proton transfer.
Aniline, acetanilide and benzoic acid have only one type of group.`},{id:"neet-2017-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Proteins: denaturation",q:"Which of the following statements is not correct?",o:["Denaturation makes the proteins more active.","Insulin maintains sugar level in the blood of a human body.","Ovalbumin is a simple food reserve in egg-white.","Blood proteins thrombin and fibrinogen are involved in blood clotting."],c:0,s:`Denaturation destroys the shape of a protein.
A protein with a damaged shape loses its biological activity.
So the statement that denaturation makes proteins more active is wrong.`}]})();
